/*
  A correction to what I suggested earlier: I've dropped aggregateRating rather
  than filling it in.

  Google's structured data policy treats reviews a business collects and
  publishes about itself as "self-serving", and self-serving reviews are not
  eligible for review snippets on LocalBusiness or Organization markup. Google
  sources the stars for a local listing from the Business Profile instead. So
  the block would very likely have been ignored, and an inflated count is a
  manual action risk for no upside.

  For reference, the real numbers as they stand: Google shows 5.0 from 9
  reviews. The "over 500" figure on the page is the Treatwell total, which is
  fine as visible copy — it just isn't something to assert as schema here.
*/

/*
  Treatment categories, reconciled line by line against the Treatwell menu
  (July 2026).

  Rules for this array:

  1. No prices, no discounts, no durations. Treatwell is the single live source
     for all three, so the website can never go stale or contradict it.
  2. Every treatment named in a description must exist as a bookable listing on
     Treatwell. If it isn't on the menu, it doesn't go here — naming treatments
     that can't be booked sends people to a dead end and is exactly the problem
     that got Hair Restoration removed.
  3. Categories follow Treatwell's own navigation (Face, Body, Massage, Hair
     Removal, Men's) so the site and the booking menu read as one journey.

  Hair Restoration was removed: it was advertised here with supporting
  before/after photography, but there is no bookable hair restoration listing
  on Treatwell. If Layla still offers it by enquiry, it should come back with a
  WhatsApp CTA rather than a Treatwell one.
*/
