/*
  Treatment clips, taken from Instagram and re-encoded for the web (18MB down
  to 1.9MB across the three).

  Self-hosted deliberately, rather than using Instagram's embed. That embed
  needs over 100KB of Meta JavaScript, loads their tracking on your site — which
  brings cookie-consent obligations under PECR — and often shows "content
  unavailable" to logged-out visitors. Self-hosting avoids all three and gives
  you control over playback.

  Nothing downloads until someone taps play: preload="none" means only the
  poster image loads with the page.

  Audio was stripped during encoding, since these play silently like social
  clips. If any of them has a voiceover worth keeping, say so and it can be
  re-encoded with sound.

  Captions name Plaxel Plasma and Collagen Lift because both are real
  treatments Layla offers. Deliberately no efficacy claims here: the Instagram
  captions talk about skin tightening, lifting and reducing hooding, and claims
  like those need substantiation under the CAP Code. The ASA has ruled against
  clinics over plasma and fibroblast claims specifically. Describing what the
  treatment is carries no such risk; describing what it will achieve does. Keep
  the outcome talk on Instagram, or get it substantiated before it goes here.

  Filenames stay as treatment-clip-N because the files are already uploaded.
  Descriptive names (collagen-lift-facial.mp4 and so on) would earn a little in
  image and video search, so worth renaming if you ever re-upload — but it is a
  marginal gain and not worth breaking working links for.

  Collagen Lift is named in the captions because it maps to a real bookable
  Treatwell listing (Collagen Lift Radio Frequency). The first clip is
  deliberately not tied to a named treatment: the device is visible but not
  identifiable with enough confidence, and naming the wrong one would send
  people to the wrong booking. Update that caption if Layla confirms it.
*/
